---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatColor from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatColor.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="hsl-to-hex">
  <DevFormatColor client:load from="hsl" to="hex" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The HSL to HEX conversion</h2>
    <p>
      HSL to RGB first finds the chroma (how much colour there is at this lightness). Then it puts the
      chroma on the correct pair of channels for the 60-degree sector of the wheel that has the hue.
    </p>
    <pre><code>hsl(221, 83%, 53%)
  C = (1 - |2L - 1|) × S     = (1 - 0.06) × 0.83 = 0.780
  X = C × (1 - |(H/60) mod 2 - 1|)               = 0.078
  m = L - C/2                                    = 0.140

  H = 221 is in the 180–240 sector → (r,g,b) = (0, X, C)
  r = (0     + 0.140) × 255 = 36
  g = (0.078 + 0.140) × 255 = 56
  b = (0.780 + 0.140) × 255 = 234

  #2438ea</code></pre>
    <p>
      All intermediate values stay in floating point and are rounded only at the last step. So a hex code
      converted to HSL and back returns the original byte.
    </p>

    <h2>What you can paste</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>hsl(221, 83%, 53%)</code>: the comma syntax.</li>
      <li><code>hsl(221 83% 53% / 50%)</code>: the modern space syntax with alpha.</li>
      <li><code>hsla(221, 83%, 53%, 0.5)</code>: alpha as 0 to 1.</li>
      <li><code>221 83% 53%</code>: a bare triple with percent signs, as a design token often stores it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Hue wraps, so 380 is treated as 20 and −40 as 320. Saturation and lightness are clamped to 0–100.
    </p>

    <h2>Why your hex might differ by one</h2>
    <p>
      HSL is continuous and hex is a grid of 16.7 million points, so the conversion snaps to the nearest
      hex colour. If you round a hue to a whole degree before you convert, a channel can move by one:
      <code>hsl(220.9, …)</code> and <code>hsl(221, …)</code> are slightly different colours. To get an
      exact hex value, convert in the other direction and copy the full-precision HSL from the table,
      not the rounded display value.
    </p>

    <h2>Storing HSL in a design system</h2>
    <p>
      A useful pattern: store the three channel values without the function wrapper in a custom property,
      then use them where needed.
    </p>
    <pre><code>:root &#123;
  --brand: 221 83% 53%;
&#125;

.button &#123;
  background: hsl(var(--brand));
&#125;
.button:hover &#123;
  background: hsl(221 83% 45%);
&#125;
.button-tint &#123;
  background: hsl(var(--brand) / 12%);
&#125;</code></pre>
    <p>
      That gives one source of truth for the hue, and alpha variants, without a preprocessor. The same
      pattern works with <code>rgb()</code> and space-separated channels.
    </p>

    <h2>What about oklch?</h2>
    <p>
      Oklch is better for scales, because equal lightness steps look equal across different hues, and
      HSL steps do not. This converter does not output it. A correct sRGB↔Oklab conversion needs a gamma
      transfer function and a matrix multiply, and a slightly wrong result is worse than none. Convert to
      hex here, and use a dedicated Oklch tool for wide-gamut work.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
